 "rulebook": "CrossFit Games movement standard, toes-to-bar: from a full hang with the arms extended and the heels behind the bar, both feet touch the bar at the same time between the hands; kipping is allowed. The bar is the FIG horizontal bar (FIG Apparatus Norms: 2.80 m above the floor, 2.40 m between the uprights, 28 mm in diameter) over 0.20 m mats",
 "deviations": [
  "the grip (the hands on the bar): each hand is a two-finger gripper, Unitree's Dex1-1 (90 mm stroke, 80 mm jaws with rubber pads, at most 120 N; sold for the G1), closed across the bar; it holds the bar by the jaws' friction alone, nothing is welded, and the bar can slide along the jaws and out of them. Flat jaws clamp the bar but cannot wrap round it, so the bar turns freely in its bearings about its own axis in place of the hands turning round a fixed bar",
  "the grip (the hands stay on the bar): defined by contact: a hand holds the bar while the bar touches both of its gripper's jaws; a break of up to 0.05 s (the pads' contact flickering under a load; measured breaks last 1 to 3 ms) is not letting go",
  "CrossFit standard (the full hang is judged by eye: arms extended, heels behind the bar): the hang is judged by where the feet are (both feet's centres at least 1.4 m below the bar, toes-to-bar's nine tenths of its dead hang's 1.55 m, and behind the bar), because the referee has positions, not a judge's eye",
  "FIG Apparatus Norms, horizontal bar (elasticity): the bar is rigid, because the simulator has rigid bodies only"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Hanging from the high bar by the two grippers that are your hands, do five toes-to-bar: bring both feet up to touch the bar between your hands, and come back to the full hang each time, without letting go.",
  "rules": "The bar is 0.028 m thick, 2.8 m above the floor, 2.4 m long between its two uprights, along the y axis; it turns freely about its own axis. Your hands are two-finger grippers, and at the start each is closed on the bar; they hold it by the friction of their jaws only, so the bar can slide along the jaws and out of them. A hand holds the bar while the bar touches both of that gripper's jaws; when for more than 0.05 s a hand has not held it, you have let go (whether you opened the gripper or the bar slipped out), and there is no re-grip. Besides the grippers only your legs may touch the bar: an arm, the trunk or the head on it fails. A repetition has two parts, in this order. The touch: both feet touch the bar at the same moment, each at a point between your two hands (the jaws' centres; shins, knees or anything else of the legs on the bar is not a touch, and does not fail). The hang: afterwards the centres of both feet are at least 1.4 m below the bar and behind it (x less than the bar's; the heels back behind the bar), both at the same moment (in your dead hang they are 1.59 m below it). You succeed when the hang of repetition 5 is reached. Swinging is allowed (a kip is legal), as is bending the knees on the way. You fail the moment you let go of the bar, an arm, the trunk or the head touches the bar, any part of you touches the uprights, the mats or the floor, or after 60 s.",
  "venue": "At t = 0 you hang still under the middle of the bar, facing +x: both arms straight up, the wrists turned so that each gripper's jaws close fore and aft on the bar, the fingers pointing up and the bar between them 0.015 m from the gripper's body (1 mm clear of it; the jaws are 0.08 m long beyond it), the jaws' centres at y = +0.207 and -0.207 m, your legs in the standing posture, your pelvis 0.84 m below the bar, your feet 1.59 m below it and 0.03 m behind it, your soles about 1.21 m above the floor (1.01 m above the 0.20 m mats under the bar). Your feet are 0.24 m apart, each 0.08 m wide. Both grip values are 1: the clamp's full 120 N on each jaw. A grip value is that gripper's closure command (0 open, 1 closed): on the 28 mm bar, about 0.82 or more clamps at 120 N, less clamps more lightly (10 N per mm that the commanded opening, 90 mm times one less the value, is below 28 mm), and below about 0.69 the jaws no longer clamp it. The bar is not a free body: the `held` flags of your state stay empty for it; the grip is judged as the rules say. Nothing holds the legs up here: the `stand` action keeps the legs in the stance with a feed-forward meant for standing on the ground; `joints` or `lowcmd` rows drive every joint. The uprights stand at the two ends of the bar, 1.2 m to either side of you.",
  "difficulty": "The grippers' friction holds about 1.4 times your weight in all, and the bar never slides back in: a kip or a quick snap of the legs, and the feet striking the bar, pull harder than that, so the feet have to be brought up and lowered with little more than your weight on the bar. The bar is 0.84 m above your pelvis and your legs are 0.75 m long: hip flexion alone does not bring the toes to the bar, the trunk has to lean back as well, against shoulders of 25 N m, five times within the time."
 },
